{
  "fixture": "declining-balance-depreciation",
  "description": "Declining-balance depreciation, and the switch to straight line that goes with it. Until now the core knew exactly one method: straight line, spread flat across the useful life. That is not a missing convenience — where a jurisdiction offers declining balance as an option (Germany does again for movables acquired between mid-2025 and the end of 2027, and separately for new residential buildings), an embedder simply could not take it. The mechanism is one calculation used by both of those rules, so it belongs in the core once; the rate, its cap and the window it applies in are pack data, because they are exactly what differs. The rate is the lower of factor x the straight-line rate and the cap: for a five-year life that is min(3 x 20%, 30%) = 30%. The switch is automatic and taken at the point where straight line over the REMAINING life first yields more than another declining year — with 10,000.00 over five years: 3,000.00 then 2,100.00 declining, then 1,633.34 / 1,633.33 / 1,633.33, because 4,900.00 over three remaining years beats 4,900.00 x 30%. Both the rate and the remaining-life figure are taken with allocate, the same largest-remainder rule the rest of the core uses, which is where those cent differences come from. Automatic because the switch is permitted rather than required, and no bookkeeper who is allowed to take it declines to; the alternative would be an option nobody ever sets differently. Note the sum: exactly the acquisition cost, no residue, which is the property that makes the schedule usable at all.",
